  "type": "numerical",
  "level": 3,
  "question": {
    "text": "The number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>-digit integers in the closed interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mn>2022</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4482</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;formed by using the digits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></math>&nbsp;is ______.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "569",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Given the number to be formed by using the digits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></math>&nbsp;</p><p>Now assuming counting integers starting from&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math></p><p>Case-I: if zero on&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>nd</mi></msup></math>&nbsp;place</p><p>(i)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>_</mi></math>, now here at unit place&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;number can take place except zero {as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2020</mn><mo>&#60;</mo><mn>2022</mn></math>}, so&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;possible way.</p><p>(ii)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>_</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>_</mi></math>, now here at ten&#39;s place&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"}\" open=\"{\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;can take place&nbsp;&nbsp;and at unit place all&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;number can take place so total&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>24</mn></math>&nbsp;possible way</p><p>Case-II: If non-zero number on&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>nd</mi></msup></math>&nbsp;place</p><p>i.e&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><munder><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>_</mi></mrow><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder></mrow><mn>5</mn></munder><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><munder><munder><mi>_</mi><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder><mn>6</mn></munder><munder><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><munder><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>_</mi></mrow><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder></mrow><mn>6</mn></munder></math>, here at hundredth place&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn></math>&nbsp;possibility will be there and at unit and ten&#39;s place&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mn>6</mn></math>&nbsp;possibility will be there, so in total&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>180</mn></math>&nbsp;possible cases,</p><p>Case -III when counting integers starting from&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>3</mn></mtd><mtd><munder><munder><mi>_</mi><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder><mn>6</mn></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><munder><mi>_</mi><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder><mn>6</mn></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><munder><mi>_</mi><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder><mn>6</mn></munder></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>&nbsp;here total&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>216</mn></math>&nbsp;possible&nbsp;cases</p><p>Case -IV when counting integers starting from&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math></p><p>(i) If&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></math>&nbsp;or&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math>&nbsp;on&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>nd</mi></msup></math>&nbsp;place</p><p>i.e.,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><munder><munder><mi>_</mi><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder><mn>3</mn></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><munder><mi>_</mi><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder><mn>6</mn></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><munder><mi>_</mi><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder><mn>6</mn></munder></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>&nbsp;here total&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>108</mn></math>&nbsp;possible&nbsp;cases</p><p>(ii) If&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>4</mn></math>&nbsp;on&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>nd</mi></msup></math>&nbsp;place</p><p>i.e.,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>3</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>4</mn></mtd><mtd><munder><munder><mi>_</mi><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder><mn>6</mn></munder></mtd><mtd><munder><munder><mi>_</mi><mo>&#9183;</mo></munder><mn>6</mn></munder></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>&nbsp;here total&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>36</mn></math>&nbsp;possible&nbsp;cases</p><p>So, total possible cases will be&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>24</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>180</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>216</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>108</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>36</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>569</mn></math>&nbsp;numbers</p></blockquote>",
